(json's unit tests - the library gating itself, in this repo, with Vox as
 its only dependency.

 Where json_demo.vox shows the library being used, this shows it being
 held to account: many small named claims, each one checked, and a tally
 at the end. A claim that does not hold prints

   ASSERT <name>: expected <x> got <y>

 and the program exits 1, so `make test` fails on it. Nothing else is
 printed but the section headings and the tally, which is why the expected
 transcript is short: silence is the whole point of a passing test.

 One thing to know before reading the JSON below. A brace in a Vox text
 begins a format hole, so a literal brace is written twice: `{{` for `{`
 and `}}` for `}`. That doubling is Vox's rule about its own texts, not
 anything to do with JSON.

   vox json.vox --shared -o libjson.so
   vox json_tests.vox --link json -o json_tests
   ./json_tests)

see json version "0.1" from "./libjson.lib".

a number called 'assertions made' is 0.
a number called failures is 0.

(Every claim goes through here, so a failure always reads the same way and
 the tally at the end is the whole verdict.)
To check with a text called name and a text called wanted and a text called got.
  increment 'assertions made'.
  If got is wanted then, Return.
  increment failures.
  Print "ASSERT {name}: expected {wanted} got {got}".

(Read a document and write it straight back out in the compact form: what
 comes back is what the reader made of it, said in one text.)
To 'read back' with a text called document.
  Return a text, 'to json' of {'from json' of document}.

(A document the reader must refuse, and one it must accept. Both go through
 check so that every claim lands in the same tally.)
To 'must refuse' with a text called name and a text called document.
  a boolean called 'it read as json' is 'reads as json' of document.
  a text called verdict is "accepted".
  If 'it read as json' is false then, set verdict to "refused".
  check with name and "refused" and verdict.

To 'must accept' with a text called name and a text called document.
  a boolean called 'it read as json' is 'reads as json' of document.
  a text called verdict is "refused".
  If 'it read as json' then, set verdict to "accepted".
  check with name and "accepted" and verdict.

(A canonical document must come back byte for byte: written tightest, read,
 and written tightest again.)
To 'must round trip' with a text called name and a text called document.
  check with name and document and {'read back' with document}.

Print "-- writing scalars --".
check with "an integer" and "42" and {'to json' of 42}.
check with "a negative integer" and "-7" and {'to json' of -7}.
check with "zero" and "0" and {'to json' of 0}.
check with "a decimal" and "3.25" and {'to json' of 3.25}.
check with "a whole decimal keeps its point" and "2.0" and {'to json' of 2.0}.
check with "a negative decimal" and "-0.5" and {'to json' of -0.5}.
check with "true" and "true" and {'to json' of true}.
check with "false" and "false" and {'to json' of false}.
check with "nothing is null" and "null" and {'to json' of nothing}.
check with "a text" and "\"plain\"" and {'to json' of "plain"}.
check with "the empty text" and "\"\"" and {'to json' of ""}.
check with "an empty list" and "[]" and {'to json' of []}.
check with "an empty map" and "{{}}" and {'to json' of {{}}}.
check with "a list of one" and "[1]" and {'to json' of [1]}.
check with "a mixed list" and "[1,\"two\",3.5,true,false,null]" and {'to json' of [1, "two", 3.5, true, false, nothing]}.
check with "a nested list" and "[[1],[2,[3]]]" and {'to json' of [[1],[2,[3]]]}.

Print "-- writing escapes --".
check with "a quote is escaped" and "\"a\\\"b\"" and {'to json' of "a\"b"}.
check with "a backslash is escaped" and "\"a\\\\b\"" and {'to json' of "a\\b"}.
check with "a newline is escaped" and "\"a\\nb\"" and {'to json' of "a\nb"}.
check with "a tab is escaped" and "\"a\\tb\"" and {'to json' of "a\tb"}.
Create a buffer called 'one odd byte'.
set byte 1 of 'one odd byte' to 13.
a text called 'a carriage return' is "{'one odd byte'}".
check with "a carriage return is escaped" and "\"\\r\"" and {'to json' of 'a carriage return'}.
set byte 1 of 'one odd byte' to 8.
a text called 'a backspace' is "{'one odd byte'}".
check with "a backspace is escaped" and "\"\\b\"" and {'to json' of 'a backspace'}.
set byte 1 of 'one odd byte' to 12.
a text called 'a form feed' is "{'one odd byte'}".
check with "a form feed is escaped" and "\"\\f\"" and {'to json' of 'a form feed'}.
set byte 1 of 'one odd byte' to 1.
a text called 'a start of heading' is "{'one odd byte'}".
check with "a lettered escape is not invented" and "\"\\u0001\"" and {'to json' of 'a start of heading'}.
set byte 1 of 'one odd byte' to 31.
a text called 'a unit separator' is "{'one odd byte'}".
check with "the last control byte" and "\"\\u001f\"" and {'to json' of 'a unit separator'}.
set byte 1 of 'one odd byte' to 32.
a text called 'a space' is "{'one odd byte'}".
check with "a space is not a control byte" and "\" \"" and {'to json' of 'a space'}.
check with "a slash stays bare" and "\"a/b\"" and {'to json' of "a/b"}.
check with "an accented letter passes through" and "\"café\"" and {'to json' of "café"}.
check with "an emoji passes through" and "\"😀\"" and {'to json' of "😀"}.

Print "-- reading escapes --".
check with "an escaped quote" and "\"a\\\"b\"" and {'read back' with "\"a\\\"b\""}.
check with "an escaped backslash" and "\"a\\\\b\"" and {'read back' with "\"a\\\\b\""}.
check with "an escaped slash comes back bare" and "\"a/b\"" and {'read back' with "\"a\\/b\""}.
check with "the five lettered control escapes" and "\"\\b\\f\\n\\r\\t\"" and {'read back' with "\"\\b\\f\\n\\r\\t\""}.
check with "a hex escape of a control byte" and "\"\\u0001\"" and {'read back' with "\"\\u0001\""}.
check with "a hex escape in capitals" and "\"\\u001f\"" and {'read back' with "\"\\u001F\""}.
check with "a hex escape of a letter" and "\"A\"" and {'read back' with "\"\\u0041\""}.
check with "a hex escape above ASCII" and "\"café\"" and {'read back' with "\"caf\\u00e9\""}.
check with "a hex escape of a CJK letter" and "\"中\"" and {'read back' with "\"\\u4e2d\""}.
check with "a surrogate pair is one character" and "\"😀\"" and {'read back' with "\"\\ud83d\\ude00\""}.
check with "a surrogate pair among letters" and "\"a😀b\"" and {'read back' with "\"a\\ud83d\\ude00b\""}.
'must refuse' with "a null escape, which a Vox text cannot hold" and "\"\\u0000\"".
'must refuse' with "a lone leading surrogate" and "\"\\ud83d\"".
'must refuse' with "a lone trailing surrogate" and "\"\\ude00\"".
'must refuse' with "a leading surrogate followed by a letter" and "\"\\ud83dA\"".
'must refuse' with "a hex escape cut short" and "\"\\u12\"".
'must refuse' with "a hex escape with a bad digit" and "\"\\u12g4\"".
'must refuse' with "an escape that is not one of the nine" and "\"a\\xb\"".
'must refuse' with "a backslash at the very end" and "\"a\\\"".

Print "-- numbers --".
check with "eighteen digits stay a whole number" and "999999999999999999" and {'read back' with "999999999999999999"}.
check with "seventeen digits stay a whole number" and "9007199254740993" and {'read back' with "9007199254740993"}.
check with "nineteen digits become a decimal" and "1000000000000000000.0" and {'read back' with "1000000000000000000"}.
check with "the rule counts digits, not whether it would fit" and "9223372036854775808.0" and {'read back' with "9223372036854775807"}.
check with "twenty digits become a decimal" and "10000000000000000000.0" and {'read back' with "10000000000000000000"}.
check with "a very long integer becomes a decimal" and "1234567890123456824475648.0" and {'read back' with "1234567890123456789012345"}.
check with "an exponent moves the point exactly" and "100.0" and {'read back' with "1e2"}.
check with "a capital exponent" and "100.0" and {'read back' with "1E2"}.
check with "an exponent with a plus" and "100.0" and {'read back' with "1e+2"}.
check with "a fraction with an exponent" and "1500.0" and {'read back' with "1.5e3"}.
check with "a negative exponent" and "0.01" and {'read back' with "1e-2"}.
check with "a fraction and a negative exponent" and "0.025" and {'read back' with "2.5E-2"}.
check with "a negative number with a negative exponent" and "-0.0015" and {'read back' with "-1.5e-3"}.
check with "minus zero is zero" and "0" and {'read back' with "-0"}.
check with "zero" and "0" and {'read back' with "0"}.
check with "zero with a fraction" and "0.5" and {'read back' with "0.5"}.
check with "a negative fraction" and "-0.25" and {'read back' with "-0.25"}.
'must refuse' with "a leading zero" and "01".
'must refuse' with "a leading zero on a negative number" and "-01".
'must refuse' with "a bare decimal point" and "1.".
'must refuse' with "a point with no whole part" and ".5".
'must refuse' with "a lone minus" and "-".
'must refuse' with "an exponent with no digits" and "1e".
'must refuse' with "an exponent with only a sign" and "1e+".
'must refuse' with "an exponent too long to mean anything" and "1e999999".
'must refuse' with "a number past what a decimal holds" and "1e400".
'must refuse' with "a leading plus" and "+1".
'must accept' with "a number at the edge of the range" and "1e308".

Print "-- null, true, and false --".
'must round trip' with "null on its own" and "null".
'must round trip' with "true on its own" and "true".
'must round trip' with "false on its own" and "false".
'must round trip' with "the three of them in a list" and "[true,false,null]".
'must refuse' with "True with a capital" and "True".
'must refuse' with "NULL with capitals" and "NULL".
'must refuse' with "NaN, which JSON does not have" and "NaN".
'must refuse' with "Infinity, which JSON does not have" and "Infinity".
'must refuse' with "a bare word" and "hello".
a value called 'a document with a null' is 'from json' of "{{\"here\":null}}".
a map called 'the null holder' is 'a document with a null'.
a value called 'the null itself' is 'the null holder''s "here".
a text called 'what the null is' is "a value".
If 'the null itself' is nothing then, set 'what the null is' to "nothing".
check with "a null key holds nothing" and "nothing" and 'what the null is'.
check with "a null key is still present" and "1" and "{'the null holder''s length}".

Print "-- objects and arrays --".
'must round trip' with "an empty object" and "{{}}".
'must round trip' with "an empty array" and "[]".
'must round trip' with "one entry" and "{{\"a\":1}}".
'must round trip' with "keys in the order they were written" and "{{\"z\":1,\"a\":2,\"m\":3}}".
'must round trip' with "an empty key" and "{{\"\":1}}".
'must round trip' with "a key with a space" and "{{\"a b\":1}}".
'must round trip' with "a key with braces in it" and "{{\"{{}}\":1}}".
'must round trip' with "a key with a quote in it" and "{{\"\\\"\":1}}".
'must round trip' with "an object inside an array inside an object" and "{{\"a\":[{{\"b\":[1,2]}}]}}".
'must round trip' with "empty collections inside a document" and "{{\"a\":[],\"b\":{{}},\"c\":[[]],\"d\":{{\"e\":{{}}}}}}".
check with "a later key of the same name wins" and "{{\"a\":2}}" and {'read back' with "{{\"a\":1,\"a\":2}}"}.
'must refuse' with "an unquoted key" and "{{a: 1}}".
'must refuse' with "a number as a key" and "{{1: 2}}".
'must refuse' with "a missing colon" and "{{\"a\" 1}}".
'must refuse' with "a missing value" and "{{\"a\":}}".
'must refuse' with "a trailing comma in an object" and "{{\"a\":1,}}".
'must refuse' with "a trailing comma in an array" and "[1,2,]".
'must refuse' with "a doubled comma" and "[1,,2]".
'must refuse' with "an object cut short" and "{{\"a\":1".
'must refuse' with "an array cut short" and "[1,2".
'must refuse' with "a string cut short" and "\"abc".
'must refuse' with "a closing brace on its own" and "}}".
'must refuse' with "a closing bracket on its own" and "]".
'must refuse' with "the wrong closing bracket" and "[1}}".
'must refuse' with "single quotes around a string" and "'a'".
'must refuse' with "a raw newline inside a string" and "\"a\nb\"".
'must refuse' with "a raw tab inside a string" and "\"a\tb\"".

Print "-- whole documents --".
'must refuse' with "nothing at all" and "".
'must refuse' with "whitespace and nothing else" and "  \t\n ".
'must refuse' with "two values where one was asked for" and "1 2".
'must refuse' with "text left over after the document" and "{{\"a\":1}} extra".
'must accept' with "whitespace before and after" and "  \n\t {{\"a\":1}} \n  ".
check with "whitespace between every token is ignored" and "{{\"a\":[1,2]}}" and {'read back' with " {{ \"a\" : [ 1 , 2 ] }} "}.
a value called 'a malformed answer' is 'from json' of "{{\"a\":1".
a text called 'what a malformed document answers' is "a value".
If 'a malformed answer' is nothing then, set 'what a malformed document answers' to "nothing".
check with "a malformed document answers nothing" and "nothing" and 'what a malformed document answers'.

Print "-- nesting depth --".
Create a buffer called 'deep arrays'.
Create a buffer called 'deeper arrays'.
Create a buffer called 'deep objects'.
Create a buffer called 'deeper objects'.
a number called level is 1.
While level is less than or equal to 64, append "[" to 'deep arrays', append "{{\"a\":" to 'deep objects', increment level.
append "1" to 'deep arrays'.
append "1" to 'deep objects'.
set level to 1.
While level is less than or equal to 64, append "]" to 'deep arrays', append "}}" to 'deep objects', increment level.
set level to 1.
While level is less than or equal to 65, append "[" to 'deeper arrays', append "{{\"a\":" to 'deeper objects', increment level.
append "1" to 'deeper arrays'.
append "1" to 'deeper objects'.
set level to 1.
While level is less than or equal to 65, append "]" to 'deeper arrays', append "}}" to 'deeper objects', increment level.
'must accept' with "ten arrays deep" and "[[[[[[[[[[1]]]]]]]]]]".
'must accept' with "sixty-four arrays deep" and "{'deep arrays'}".
'must refuse' with "sixty-five arrays deep" and "{'deeper arrays'}".
'must accept' with "sixty-four objects deep" and "{'deep objects'}".
'must refuse' with "sixty-five objects deep" and "{'deeper objects'}".
a map called 'a map that holds itself' is {{}}.
Set 'a map that holds itself's "self" to 'a map that holds itself'.
check with "a map that holds itself writes nothing" and "" and {'to json' of 'a map that holds itself'}.
a list called 'a list that holds itself' is [].
append 'a list that holds itself' to 'a list that holds itself'.
check with "a list that holds itself writes nothing" and "" and {'to json' of 'a list that holds itself'}.

Print "-- laying a document out --".
a text called 'the compact document' is "{{\"name\":\"Ada\",\"tags\":[\"x\",\"y\"],\"here\":{{}},\"none\":[],\"score\":9.5}}".
a value called 'the document' is 'from json' of 'the compact document'.
a text called 'laid out by two' is "{{\n  \"name\": \"Ada\",\n  \"tags\": [\n    \"x\",\n    \"y\"\n  ],\n  \"here\": {{}},\n  \"none\": [],\n  \"score\": 9.5\n}}".
check with "indented by two" and 'laid out by two' and {'to json with indent' of 'the document' and 2}.
a text called 'laid out by four' is "{{\n    \"name\": \"Ada\",\n    \"tags\": [\n        \"x\",\n        \"y\"\n    ],\n    \"here\": {{}},\n    \"none\": [],\n    \"score\": 9.5\n}}".
check with "indented by four" and 'laid out by four' and {'to json with indent' of 'the document' and 4}.
a text called 'laid out by none' is "{{\n\"name\": \"Ada\",\n\"tags\": [\n\"x\",\n\"y\"\n],\n\"here\": {{}},\n\"none\": [],\n\"score\": 9.5\n}}".
check with "a width of zero still breaks the lines" and 'laid out by none' and {'to json with indent' of 'the document' and 0}.
check with "a width below zero reads as zero" and 'laid out by none' and {'to json with indent' of 'the document' and -3}.
check with "an empty object stays on one line" and "{{}}" and {'to json with indent' of {{}} and 2}.
check with "an empty array stays on one line" and "[]" and {'to json with indent' of [] and 2}.
check with "a number has no layout to give it" and "42" and {'to json with indent' of 42 and 2}.
check with "a text has no layout to give it" and "\"plain\"" and {'to json with indent' of "plain" and 2}.
check with "nothing has no layout to give it" and "null" and {'to json with indent' of nothing and 2}.
a text called 'a nested layout' is "[\n  1,\n  [\n    2\n  ]\n]".
check with "an array inside an array" and 'a nested layout' and {'to json with indent' of [1, [2]] and 2}.
a text called 'the laid-out document' is 'to json with indent' of 'the document' and 2.
check with "a laid-out document reads back the same" and 'the compact document' and {'read back' with 'the laid-out document'}.
'must accept' with "a laid-out document is a document" and 'the laid-out document'.
check with "'to json' is untouched by all this" and 'the compact document' and {'to json' of 'the document'}.

Print "-- round trips --".
'must round trip' with "a whole number" and "42".
'must round trip' with "a decimal" and "3.5".
'must round trip' with "a text" and "\"plain\"".
'must round trip' with "escapes of every kind" and "\"\\\"\\\\\\b\\f\\n\\r\\t\"".
'must round trip' with "a hex escape that has to stay one" and "\"\\u0001\"".
'must round trip' with "letters above ASCII" and "\"café中😀\"".
'must round trip' with "a realistic document" and "{{\"a\":1,\"b\":[1,2],\"c\":{{\"d\":null}},\"e\":\"f\",\"g\":true,\"h\":-2.5}}".
'must round trip' with "a document of nothing but empties" and "[[],{{}},[{{}}],{{\"a\":[]}}]".
a text called 'twice round' is 'read back' with {'read back' with "{{\"a\":[1,\"two\",null,true]}}"}.
check with "reading and writing twice changes nothing" and "{{\"a\":[1,\"two\",null,true]}}" and 'twice round'.

Print "{'assertions made'} assertions, {failures} failures".
If failures is greater than 0 then, exit 1.
